# {{ ansible_managed }}
# =============================================================================
# Curated alarm — a container failing its healthcheck (ADR-030, phase 2)
# =============================================================================
# Replaces the "unhealthy >10min" check in homelab-health.sh, which the heal
# timer cannot cover: that timer only resurrects containers that EXITED, so one
# that stays up while failing its healthcheck is invisible to it. The real gap
# is internal containers — nextcloud-notify-push above all, whose death silently
# kills mobile push while Nextcloud itself keeps answering 200.
#
# --- Why the ten minutes live in the lookup and not in `delay` ---------------
# This is the thing to get right, and it is not obvious.
#
# `delay: up 10m` postpones the NOTIFICATION, not the status transition. The
# alarm goes WARNING immediately and the API reports it immediately. Netdata
# notifies nobody here — the host-side adapter READS the status — so a delay
# would have been decoration, and the ten-minute gate that homelab-health.sh
# implements with a stamp file would have been silently lost.
#
# `min -10m of unhealthy` puts the gate in the VALUE instead: it is 1 only if
# the dimension was 1 at every point of the window, i.e. the container has been
# unhealthy continuously for ten minutes. A container is briefly unhealthy on
# every restart, and `starting` is a separate dimension that this deliberately
# does not match — same two decisions as the script, arrived at the same way.
#
# Recovery is immediate and that is deliberate: one healthy sample drops the
# minimum to 0. No hysteresis, because unlike a temperature sitting on a
# threshold, a healthcheck does not oscillate around a line.
#
# --- What this alarm cannot see ---------------------------------------------
# A container that does not EXIST produces no chart, so no instance of this
# template exists to fail. That half is covered elsewhere and on purpose: the
# goss spec (ADR-032) runs `docker inspect <name>` for every service in
# compose.yaml, so an absent container fails its assertion there. Different
# latency — daily rather than every five minutes — and that difference is the
# honest cost of the split.
#
# PHASE 2 HAS DELETED. This line read "PHASE 2 DELETES NOTHING" until 2026-08-29
# and had been false since 2026-08-26. The replacement was observed firing on
# 2026-08-24 01:45 — a deliberately unhealthy throwaway container, NAMED in the
# push rather than counted — and c2c2a36 then removed this check from
# homelab-health.sh. ADR-030's migration rule completing, not being waived; the
# tombstone at the foot of homelab-health.sh carries the dates.
# =============================================================================

# --- The gate the lookup could not provide on its own (#262) -----------------
# `min -10m` is TRUE for a container that has existed for one minute and been
# unhealthy for all of it. The window is only as long as the chart, and a
# recreated container gets a brand-new chart. Measured over every transition
# netdata retained: twelve raises, and TEN of them came from UNINITIALIZED —
# collabora, calibre-web, immich-server and immich-ml, seconds after a deploy
# recreated them, clearing again after 233 to 508 s.
#
# Two fixes were tried against the recorded data before this one, and both were
# refuted by it:
#   - `null2zero` on the lookup. The pre-creation period yields no slots to
#     convert, so the aggregate is computed over the samples that exist. At the
#     08:15 raise it returns 1 with the option and 1 without.
#   - a `starting`-aware condition. The `starting` dimension was 0 throughout
#     the whole episode; the container went straight to unhealthy on the new
#     chart. There was no `starting` to key on.
#
# What separates them is the SAMPLE COUNT, so that is what the floor is built
# on. `homelab_container_health_samples` sums every dimension over the same
# window; exactly one dimension is 1 per sample, so its value is the number of
# samples the window actually holds. Measured on the recorded data:
#
#   collabora 08:15, spurious   min = 1   samples =  53   -> now suppressed
#   collabora 08:17, spurious   min = 1   samples = 173   -> now suppressed
#   nextcloud, the JIT outage   min = 1   samples = 660   -> still fires
#
# (Those three come from the data API over the recorded windows, where a full
# window reads 600-660. The alarm's own lookup reads 556 for the same thing —
# see the floor below. The gap is the aligned-edge difference between the two,
# and it is why the floor is set from the alarm's value and not the API's.)
#
# The floor is 500, and the number it is derived from was MEASURED on the
# deployed alarm rather than computed. A saturated window reads **556 to 568**,
# not the 600 a ten-minute window at `update_every: 1` suggests: netdata's
# health lookup aligns to the last completed update and drops the incomplete
# edges. The first attempt used 540 — arithmetic from 600 — which left sixteen
# samples of margin against a value nobody had checked.
#
# Both halves of that range are real. Within one health reload the value is
# rigid: 556 across all 28 containers, sampled over ninety seconds, identical
# every time. Across the NEXT reload it settled at 568. So it is stable enough
# to derive a floor from and not stable enough to sit close to — which is
# exactly the mistake 540 was.
#
# So the floor is about ninety per cent of the low end of that range: it needs
# roughly nine minutes of chart history, absorbs both the reload-to-reload drift
# and about a minute of collection gaps, and sits nearly three times above the
# largest spurious count ever recorded (173 s of chart).
#
# The remaining coupling is the collector's resolution, and it is the one thing
# here that could fail silently — a slower collector would never reach the floor
# and this alarm would simply stop firing.
#
# That coupling is no longer written twice. The floor is DERIVED from
# `netdata_docker_update_every`: a ten-minute window nominally holds
# 600 / update_every samples, and the floor is 83% of that, which reproduces the
# measured 500 exactly at 1 s and gives 100 at 5 s. Both sides of the ratio
# divide, so the margin against the largest spurious count ever recorded (173 s
# of chart) stays 2.9x whatever the resolution.
#
# The goss spec used to assert `update_every == 1` — a PROXY for the floor, and
# the wrong one: it would have gone red on a legitimate resolution change and
# stayed green on any other way of making the floor unreachable. It now asserts
# the resolution against the configured value AND that the floor is actually
# reachable on a mature chart.
#
# The summary now says what is enforced. It claimed ten minutes while the alarm
# could fire on one, and the one episode that DID last past ten minutes was the
# 2026-08-27 nextcloud JIT outage — 5 h 53 m, raised from CLEAR on a mature
# chart, which is the shape this alarm is for.
 template: homelab_container_health_samples
       on: docker.container_health_status
    class: Errors
     type: Docker
component: Container
   lookup: sum -10m unaligned of healthy,unhealthy,starting,no_healthcheck,not_running_unhealthy
    units: samples
    every: 1m
     info: Samples the ten-minute window actually holds — a value carrier for the alarm below, never an alarm itself
       to: silent

 template: homelab_container_unhealthy
       on: docker.container_health_status
    class: Errors
     type: Docker
component: Container
   lookup: min -10m unaligned of unhealthy
    units: boolean
    every: 1m
     warn: $this == 1 AND $homelab_container_health_samples > {{ ((600 / netdata_docker_update_every) * 0.833) | round | int }}
  summary: A container has been unhealthy across a ten-minute window with at least nine minutes of samples
     info: Container healthcheck failing continuously for 10m, on a chart old enough to prove it — the heal timer cannot see this, it only restarts containers that exited
       to: silent
